 {:group "Patterns: dispatch tables and rules"
  :entries
  [{:name samizdat.symbolic/rule
    :summary "Compile {:when pattern :if guard :then template}. A map is an open subset (extra keys ignored, a named key must be present), a vector is closed and positional, ?x binds and repeats as equal, _ matches anything, sets and lists are literals."}
   {:name samizdat.symbolic/first-match
    :summary "The first rule that matches a term, with its bindings and result — a manifest dispatch table is this over the data map."}
   {:name samizdat.symbolic/guard-catalog
    :summary "Every guard a rule or a dispatch entry may name, with its arity and meaning. Nothing outside this list evaluates."}
   {:name samizdat.symbolic/subsumes?
    :summary "Whether every term one pattern matches the other matches too — the specificity order the dispatch check uses."}
   {:name samizdat.symbolic.dispatch/analyse
    :summary "A dispatch table's shadowed branches (refused at save) and order-dependent pairs (reported), over the order that runs."}
   {:name samizdat.repl.guard/rules
    :summary "The eval guard's rules — what an eval may not do in the live image — with the clauses that decide each. Enumerable, not editable: a guard the guarded thing can edit is not a guard."}
   {:name samizdat.security.policy/rules
    :summary "The shell policy as data: the structural rules, most authoritative first, and the allow/deny table. Every refusal names the one that made it."}
   {:name samizdat.security.policy/shell-split
    :summary "How the policy reads a command's shell structure — statements, separators, redirection — through a POSIX-subset grammar; a string the shell would refuse comes back with the position it stopped at."}]}

 {:group "Policy as data"
  :entries
  [{:name samizdat.agent.gates/threshold
    :summary "Read one tunable from gates.edn. Every gate budget in the harness is a value here."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.gates/reload-config!
    :summary "Re-read gates.edn from userspace and recompile the steer table. The policy tool's save does this for you."}
   {:name samizdat.userspace/save!
    :summary "Append a new version of any userspace piece (:cell :manifest :policy :prompt), with a rationale the history shows. Prefer the matching tool, which validates first and requires the rationale."}
   {:name samizdat.userspace/revert!
    :summary "Re-append an older version of a userspace piece as the newest — the universal rollback, recorded with your stated reason."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.phases/withholds
    :summary "Which tools a phase refuses, from phases.edn."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.gates/storm-policy
    :summary "The storm guard's live policy: window, thresholds, and tool classification for withholding repeated identical calls. Tune the :storm-* thresholds and the :storm-exempt/:storm-mutating vocabularies in gates.edn; the withhold rules themselves are the phases.edn :storm entries."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.storm/blocked?
    :summary "Whether the next call with this signature would be withheld as a repeat, judged against a branch's storm window."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.storm/oscillating?
    :summary "Whether a call would extend an A-B-A-B alternation between two calls — the loop shape no failure-keyed guard can see."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.tournament/run
    :summary "Rank N candidates by pairwise comparison at linear cost: a ring pass that cancels positional bias, then everyone against the top-k pivots. The comparator is yours to inject — an LLM judge, a test result, anything returning p(a beats b)."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.trajectory/score-run
    :summary "Score a branch's trajectory from the journal on the A-T letter scale, steps-so-far only — the offline validation entry point. Run it over a completed and an exhausted run and compare curves before wiring anything to the number."}
   {:name samizdat.agent.gates/trajectory-policy
    :summary "The trajectory-scoring policy from gates.edn: repeats, stride, criteria, and the not-yet-consulted abandon threshold."}
   {:name samizdat.manifests/validate-mode
    :summary "What a cell whose data does not match its declared shape costs at RUN time, from gates.edn :schema-validation :mode — :warn (record it on :mycelium/warnings and carry on), :strict (stop with the schema error), :off. Every cell declares :input/:output and every manifest an :input-schema, so mycelium checks the shapes each pass; this decides the cost. It does NOT reach the schema CHAIN check, which refuses a manifest whose wiring does not hold together whatever this says — that half is not yours to switch off."}
   {:name samizdat.prompt/render
    :summary "Render a prompt resource through selmer. Every harness message the model sees comes through here."}]}

 {:group "Reading and writing Clojure"
  :entries
  [{:name samizdat.lisp/diagnose
    :summary "Why a piece of Clojure would not load AS WRITTEN, or nil — reason plus 1-based line/col, naming both ends of a mismatch. Answers 'is this loadable now', which is a different question from lisp/balance's 'could this be repaired': the delimiters balancing does not promise it READS, and `(defn f [] :)` is perfectly balanced and is not Clojure."}
   {:name samizdat.lisp/balance
    :summary "The repair half: closes a trailing truncation or trims a trailing over-close when the result reads, and refuses anything mid-file because closing it would re-parent code. Returns data, not sentences — the words live in prompts/file-tool.md."}
   {:name samizdat.lisp/first-odd-quote-line
    :summary "The line where the running unescaped-quote count first goes odd — the line that most likely opened a string nobody closed, which is NOT where the reader complains."}
   {:name samizdat.hashline/line-anchor
    :summary "Mint a `<line>:<hash>` anchor: the line locates, the three-hex content hash of the trimmed line verifies. What read_file({anchors: true}) prints and the patch tool spends."}
   {:name samizdat.hashline/apply-edits
    :summary "Apply a batch of anchored edits atomically. Every edit resolves against the same read, so order does not matter; one stale anchor or two edits over a line refuses the whole batch rather than half-writing it."}
   {:name samizdat.hashline/resolve-one
    :summary "The refusal ladder behind an anchor: exact, drifted-so-follow-the-content, ambiguous-hash-so-the-explicit-line-wins, far-away-so-REFUSE, and gone-but-here-is-the-current-anchor. A false refuse costs one re-read; a false accept corrupts the file."}]}

